WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.520 --> 00:00:05.792
Imagine two similar women, who&nbsp;
each have unwanted pregnancies.

00:00:05.792 --> 00:00:07.520
They both go to abortion clinics.

00:00:08.400 --> 00:00:13.360
One has a pregnancy that’s just days&nbsp;before 
the clinic’s gestational limit.

00:00:13.360 --> 00:00:15.920
And the other is just days past the limit.

00:00:16.710 --> 00:00:18.752
So one of them receives an abortion.

00:00:18.752 --> 00:00:20.638
And the other is turned away.

00:00:21.688 --> 00:00:25.688
Their different life trajectories after that&nbsp;
might not tell you a lot on their own...
 &nbsp;

00:00:26.400 --> 00:00:29.750
but if you were to track this&nbsp;
scenario for hundreds of women,&nbsp;

00:00:29.750 --> 00:00:32.560
you could gather a lot of&nbsp;
data on what life looks like

00:00:32.560 --> 00:00:34.531
after women are denied an abortion.

00:00:36.366 --> 00:00:40.000
This was the concept for a huge&nbsp;
naturalized experiment in the US&nbsp;

00:00:40.000 --> 00:00:41.840
that tracked this for the first time.

00:00:42.912 --> 00:00:46.480
For five years, every six months,&nbsp;
researchers called the women&nbsp;

00:00:46.480 --> 00:00:48.240
to see how their lives had progressed.

00:00:49.104 --> 00:00:54.144
And one of the things their research uncovered
was the financial impact of forced parenthood.

00:00:55.430 --> 00:01:00.950
What they heard from these calls reveals the
high economic cost of an abortion ban

00:01:00.950 --> 00:01:03.467
and exactly who is made to bear that cost.

00:01:04.470 --> 00:01:04.970
Hello?

00:01:10.320 --> 00:01:16.173
There's no way that you don't have a child
and love and want that child.

00:01:16.173 --> 00:01:18.893
And I very much feel that way about my son.

00:01:18.893 --> 00:01:21.840
But that isn't how we began.

00:01:21.840 --> 00:01:25.952
I felt like I was drowning and&nbsp;
had zero control over my life&nbsp;

00:01:25.952 --> 00:01:28.526
and having an abortion felt&nbsp;
like reaching out for air.

00:01:29.200 --> 00:01:33.067
The voices you hear in this&nbsp;
video weren’t part of the study.

00:01:33.067 --> 00:01:35.971
But their stories overlap&nbsp;
with some of its findings.

00:01:35.971 --> 00:01:40.667
I was six months along and so at that point
I had to carry to term.

00:01:40.667 --> 00:01:43.331
There wasn't a choice of: 
am&nbsp;I going to give birth or not?

00:01:43.331 --> 00:01:45.792
I've actually made that decision twice in life.

00:01:45.792 --> 00:01:49.273
For me, they both felt pretty easy right away.

00:01:50.470 --> 00:01:56.070
Roughly 60% of women in the study and abortion&nbsp;
seekers in the US are already parents.

00:01:57.288 --> 00:02:00.000
Abortion seekers in the study and more broadly

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:04.323
are much more likely to be a person of color
when compared to the general population.

00:02:05.000 --> 00:02:07.960
And are almost four times as&nbsp;
likely to live in poverty.

00:02:08.592 --> 00:02:12.032
And that can be about who&nbsp;
has access to contraception

00:02:12.032 --> 00:02:15.275
who has had great sex ed classes

00:02:15.275 --> 00:02:19.233
who can negotiate contraceptive use with a partner

00:02:19.233 --> 00:02:24.665
and it's also about who can&nbsp; accommodate 
a surprise pregnancy.

00:02:25.731 --> 00:02:29.011
Diana Greene Foster is a&nbsp;
demographer who led the study,&nbsp;

00:02:29.011 --> 00:02:30.800
which she calls The Turnaway Study.

00:02:31.830 --> 00:02:36.080
Among all women in the study,&nbsp;
there are 3 groups we’ll focus on.

00:02:36.080 --> 00:02:40.648
One group was women who got an abortion
in the first 13 weeks of pregnancy

00:02:40.648 --> 00:02:41.899
the first trimester.

00:02:42.643 --> 00:02:45.571
This stage of pregnancy is&nbsp;
when about 90% of abortions&nbsp;

00:02:45.571 --> 00:02:47.843
in the US happen in the general population.

00:02:48.600 --> 00:02:50.987
But for the study it was underrepresented

00:02:50.987 --> 00:02:55.440
and used as a control group for this group:
women who got abortions within two weeks

00:02:55.440 --> 00:03:00.144
of an abortion clinics’ gestational limit,
which averaged at the 20 week mark.

00:03:01.110 --> 00:03:04.950
This later-term abortion group is a less&nbsp;
common abortion experience in the US.

00:03:05.664 --> 00:03:09.114
But for the purposes of the&nbsp;
study, was overrepresented,&nbsp;

00:03:09.114 --> 00:03:11.326
in order to compare to this third group:

00:03:12.027 --> 00:03:15.947
women who just barely missed the gestational limit
and were denied an abortion.

00:03:16.630 --> 00:03:19.030
This last group is called the turnaway group.

00:03:20.000 --> 00:03:23.920
The Turnaway Study hinges on comparing&nbsp;
the outcomes of these two groups.

00:03:25.000 --> 00:03:28.320
But comparing the two groups of&nbsp;
women who sought later-term abortions&nbsp;

00:03:28.320 --> 00:03:32.310
to the first-trimester group&nbsp;
can tell us something, too.

00:03:32.310 --> 00:03:36.446
40% of the first-trimester group&nbsp;
were living under the poverty line.

00:03:36.446 --> 00:03:39.664
But 57% of the later-term abortion group were.

00:03:41.011 --> 00:03:46.931
Lack of money slows people down and prevents them
from moving as quickly to getting an abortion.

00:03:46.931 --> 00:03:51.360
When wealthy women have unwanted pregnancies,
they're more likely to get their abortion.

00:03:51.360 --> 00:03:53.811
And that's because they can afford to do it.

00:03:53.811 --> 00:03:57.520
They can travel, they can pay for the procedure,
they can get time off work.

00:03:58.560 --> 00:04:03.280
In fact, when the women in the study were surveyed
about their motivations for wanting an abortion

00:04:03.280 --> 00:04:06.710
“Not financially prepared”&nbsp;
was the most common response.

00:04:06.710 --> 00:04:11.967
What was going on in your life when&nbsp;
you had an unplanned pregnancy?

00:04:11.967 --> 00:04:14.880
We were barely able to scrape by at that point.

00:04:14.880 --> 00:04:17.091
And there was just not enough money.

00:04:17.091 --> 00:04:21.750
The financial situation was&nbsp;like a really
big deciding factor for me.

00:04:21.750 --> 00:04:24.710
I just graduated from undergrad.

00:04:24.710 --> 00:04:27.824
I was doing an unpaid fellowship.

00:04:27.824 --> 00:04:29.086
We live in the Bay Area.

00:04:29.086 --> 00:04:32.240
You definitely need a dual income&nbsp;
household to be able to live well.

00:04:32.240 --> 00:04:34.672
So we were not okay financially.

00:04:34.672 --> 00:04:38.832
Because of my documentation process,
I couldn't legally work.

00:04:38.832 --> 00:04:42.507
I was just starting my professional&nbsp;
career when I found out I was pregnant.

00:04:42.507 --> 00:04:46.064
I was three months into my&nbsp;
first real job out of college.

00:04:47.171 --> 00:04:50.291
One thing researchers asked the&nbsp;
study participants over the years

00:04:50.291 --> 00:04:53.360
was whether the relationship that&nbsp;
led to the pregnancy had lasted.

00:04:54.206 --> 00:04:57.644
This was to try to get an idea of&nbsp;
whether these women had support.

00:04:58.832 --> 00:05:00.960
At the time of the abortion clinic visit

00:05:00.960 --> 00:05:05.093
80% of the study participants said
they were still in this relationship.

00:05:05.627 --> 00:05:08.703
By the second year, that number fell to 60%.

00:05:08.703 --> 00:05:12.841
And by the 5th year, only about 27% of them were.

00:05:12.841 --> 00:05:17.128
Regardless of whether women in the study&nbsp;
received or were denied an abortion

00:05:17.128 --> 00:05:20.559
the likelihood of the&nbsp;relationship 
working out was low.

00:05:20.559 --> 00:05:25.000
He gets to go off and live his&nbsp;
life and forget that he's a father.

00:05:25.000 --> 00:05:29.792
While my entire life is about being&nbsp;
a mother and caretaking for this person.

00:05:29.792 --> 00:05:34.240
It is already like a lot to go&nbsp;
through unexpectedly

00:05:34.240 --> 00:05:36.731
and it's a lot to do it on my own.

00:05:37.232 --> 00:05:40.568
The high likelihood of that&nbsp;
relationship ending makes sense

00:05:40.568 --> 00:05:43.280
given that another common reason
for wanting an abortion

00:05:43.280 --> 00:05:45.929
was for “partner-related” reasons.

00:05:45.929 --> 00:05:50.000
I don't know that it would&nbsp;
have been a healthy thing

00:05:50.000 --> 00:05:54.160
for ourselves or a child to have to endure
those types of disagreements

00:05:54.160 --> 00:05:57.440
that type of conflict, that type of disconnect.

00:05:57.440 --> 00:06:01.920
Trying to measure other types of support,
researchers found that 5 years on

00:06:01.920 --> 00:06:06.267
women denied abortions were more likely
to be living alone with their children

00:06:06.267 --> 00:06:09.099
than women who received&nbsp;
abortions and had other children.

00:06:10.560 --> 00:06:12.560
It didn't look like they&nbsp;
were getting a lot of support&nbsp;

00:06:12.560 --> 00:06:15.327
from their partners or their immediate family.

00:06:15.327 --> 00:06:17.312
We looked at child support payments.

00:06:17.312 --> 00:06:20.832
On average, it was only about $20 a month.

00:06:20.832 --> 00:06:25.040
$20 in child support... that's extremely low.

00:06:25.040 --> 00:06:29.200
Yea and this is a lot of people&nbsp;
reporting getting $0 in child support

00:06:29.200 --> 00:06:33.532
and then a few people reporting getting&nbsp;
normal amounts of child support.

00:06:33.532 --> 00:06:37.920
And finally, the researchers gathered&nbsp;
financial data for these two groups...

00:06:37.920 --> 00:06:42.078
to figure out what becoming a parent&nbsp;
without much support looks like.

00:06:42.672 --> 00:06:48.032
We linked the participants in the turnaway study&nbsp;
to ten years of credit reporting agency data.

00:06:48.032 --> 00:06:50.939
And this went back before their pregnancies.

00:06:51.448 --> 00:06:54.568
Researchers looked at&nbsp;
financial distress indicators,&nbsp;

00:06:54.568 --> 00:06:57.368
like debt or evictions, in the two groups.

00:06:57.368 --> 00:07:00.480
And before the turnaway group&nbsp;
gave birth, the two groups&nbsp;

00:07:00.480 --> 00:07:03.051
were on similar economic trajectories.

00:07:03.547 --> 00:07:07.787
But after the turnaway group gave birth,
the two groups diverged

00:07:07.787 --> 00:07:11.862
and the level of financial distress for&nbsp;
the women denied an abortion spiked.

00:07:12.493 --> 00:07:16.328
We saw this big spike in financial&nbsp;problems 
in the turnaway group.

00:07:16.328 --> 00:07:21.251
Nearly 80% higher amounts of debt&nbsp;
that was 30 days or more past due.

00:07:21.251 --> 00:07:26.240
78% increase in things like&nbsp;
bankruptcies or liens or evictions&nbsp;

00:07:26.240 --> 00:07:28.902
where you're ordered to pay the&nbsp;
landlord some amount of money.

00:07:28.902 --> 00:07:30.291
And we saw a big spike in those.

00:07:30.291 --> 00:07:34.320
So some people might look at this research and say...

00:07:34.320 --> 00:07:38.771
you're going to be more financially&nbsp;
strained if you have a child&nbsp;

00:07:38.771 --> 00:07:42.240
because your life is just&nbsp;
going to be more expensive.

00:07:42.240 --> 00:07:48.164
So why is it important to look at this&nbsp;
in relation to an abortion denial?

00:07:48.164 --> 00:07:53.680
One thing we did to investigate this question
was we looked in the near-limit group.

00:07:53.680 --> 00:07:57.040
So some of the women who&nbsp;
initially obtained an abortion

00:07:57.040 --> 00:08:00.400
later went on and had a pregnancy that
resulted in a child

00:08:00.400 --> 00:08:01.800
and they gave birth.

00:08:02.630 --> 00:08:05.910
After the births of their children,
the financial distress indicators

00:08:05.910 --> 00:08:08.212
for these two groups looks like this.

00:08:08.212 --> 00:08:10.720
Which shows the more severe financial penalties

00:08:10.720 --> 00:08:16.017
for women having children when parenting is wanted
versus after an abortion denial.

00:08:17.515 --> 00:08:20.400
The women in this study were&nbsp;
seeking abortions because&nbsp;

00:08:20.400 --> 00:08:22.960
they knew that this was a bad time for them.

00:08:22.960 --> 00:08:26.800
They knew this was a time where it would&nbsp;
be potentially really, really costly

00:08:26.800 --> 00:08:30.646
and really damaging to them and&nbsp;
their economic circumstances.

00:08:31.344 --> 00:08:32.982
And not just their own:

00:08:32.982 --> 00:08:38.494
55% of the children of the women who were able
to get an abortion were living in poverty

00:08:38.494 --> 00:08:43.407
but that number rose to 72% for the
children of the women who were denied.

00:08:43.407 --> 00:08:46.240
Which is relevant to another&nbsp;
common reason study participants&nbsp;

00:08:46.240 --> 00:08:48.508
gave for wanting an abortion:

00:08:48.508 --> 00:08:51.490
the “need to focus on other children.”

00:08:51.490 --> 00:08:55.516
One of your fears, when you were deciding
whether or not to have an abortion

00:08:55.516 --> 00:08:59.288
was that you wouldn't be&nbsp;
able to afford the childcare.

00:08:59.288 --> 00:09:02.944
And I'm wondering how much of those fears
were realized for you?

00:09:02.944 --> 00:09:05.280
Oh 100% of those fears were realized from me.

00:09:05.280 --> 00:09:06.800
I don't have childcare now.

00:09:06.800 --> 00:09:10.177
There's still no way to&nbsp;
afford childcare on my salary.

00:09:12.432 --> 00:09:14.192
Something the Turnaway Study shows

00:09:14.192 --> 00:09:17.280
is that these reasons turned&nbsp;
into accurate predictions&nbsp;

00:09:17.280 --> 00:09:18.880
of what the women would struggle with

00:09:18.880 --> 00:09:22.000
after an abortion denial.

00:09:22.000 --> 00:09:25.971
Women understand the consequences&nbsp;
of unwanted pregnancy

00:09:25.971 --> 00:09:29.848
and when they're trying to decide whether
to have an abortion or have a baby

00:09:29.848 --> 00:09:33.690
they understand completely&nbsp;
what the outcomes will be.

00:09:35.990 --> 00:09:39.760
I was able to go to law school and&nbsp;
complete the degree that I had been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:39.760 --> 00:09:42.932
saying I wanted to do since I was eight years old.

00:09:42.932 --> 00:09:49.920
My life outside of being a mother has just been&nbsp;
exponentially harder and almost impossible.

00:09:49.920 --> 00:09:52.987
And there was mourning&nbsp;
for the life that I imagined.

00:09:52.987 --> 00:09:57.566
There was just no way I would have the&nbsp;
time or the money to do those things...

00:09:58.230 --> 00:10:01.916
like going to grad school&nbsp;
or moving away from family.

00:10:01.916 --> 00:10:06.240
I was able to fight a deportation&nbsp;case 
and I have a career.

00:10:06.240 --> 00:10:08.880
I was able to get out of a&nbsp;
marriage that just wasn't working

00:10:08.880 --> 00:10:12.710
because I didn't have to think about&nbsp;
how I'd impacted somebody else.

00:10:12.710 --> 00:10:19.475
I get to define my life the way that I want&nbsp;
to without having to factor in a family.

00:10:20.432 --> 00:10:23.272
If somebody tells you they're not&nbsp;
ready to carry a pregnancy to term...

00:10:23.272 --> 00:10:26.306
They're not. Believe them.

